Here is a prompt you can give Claude to review and fix the UX, typography, spacing, visual hierarchy, and consistency across the entire Dashboard and Simulator, with an enterprise application mindset.

Enterprise Dashboard & Simulator — UX/UI Review and Remediation

You are an enterprise UX architect and senior frontend engineer.

Review the entire application, specifically the Dashboard and Simulator, and identify and remediate UX/UI inconsistencies with a focus on readability, typography, information hierarchy, accessibility, density, responsiveness, and enterprise usability.

1. First — Inspect Before Changing

Do not immediately modify code.

First inspect:

* Dashboard pages
* Simulator pages
* Navigation
* Headers
* Cards
* KPI widgets
* Tables
* Forms
* Filters
* Dialogs/modals
* Tabs
* Status indicators
* Buttons
* Tooltips
* Empty/loading/error states
* Responsive layouts
* Shared CSS/theme/design-system components

Identify the existing typography, spacing, component, and layout patterns before introducing changes.

2. Establish a Consistent Typography System

Create or reuse centralized design tokens.

Use a consistent hierarchy such as:

* Application/page title: 24–28px
* Section heading: 18–20px
* Card/KPI title: 13–14px
* Primary KPI value: 24–32px
* Body text: 14px
* Secondary/supporting text: 12–13px
* Table text: 13–14px
* Form labels: 13–14px
* Button text: 13–14px
* Helper/error text: 12–13px

Do not blindly apply these values. Inspect the current application and adjust based on information density and available screen size.

Avoid excessive use of:

* 10–11px text
* all-caps text
* bold everywhere
* large KPI numbers that dominate the page
* inconsistent font weights
* arbitrary font sizes defined separately in components

3. Centralize Design Tokens

Create a single source of truth for:

* Font family
* Font sizes
* Font weights
* Line heights
* Spacing
* Border radius
* Control heights
* Card padding
* Table row height
* Colors
* Status colors
* Focus states

Prefer CSS variables/theme tokens or the application’s existing design-system mechanism.

Do not introduce duplicate styles when an existing shared component/token can be reused.

4. Dashboard UX

Optimize the Dashboard around this hierarchy:

Level 1 — Current Processing Health

Users should immediately understand:

* Current processing window
* Overall status
* Processing progress
* Amount/value affected
* Pending amount
* Failed amount
* Processing duration

Level 2 — Business KPIs

Prioritize:

* Prefund requests
* Prefund transactions
* Requested/reserved amount
* Pending amount
* Settlement transactions
* Settlement amount
* Failed amount
* Amount requiring attention

Level 3 — Exceptions

Clearly surface:

* Pending reservations
* Timeouts
* Failed transactions
* Retry exhausted
* Reconciliation required
* Processing delays

Level 4 — Detailed Diagnostics

Keep technical information available through drill-down rather than dominating the main dashboard.

Examples:

* Error codes
* MQ status
* Database issues
* Storage issues
* Pod/worker information
* Retry details
* Correlation IDs

5. KPI Card Standards

Every KPI card should have a consistent structure:

TITLE
Primary Value
Supporting context
Trend / status
Optional secondary metric

Avoid inconsistent card heights, padding, font sizes, or alignment.

Example:

Pending Amount
$1.82M
42 transactions
↑ 8.4% vs previous window

Do not make every piece of information visually equal.

6. Tables

Review all tables for:

* Column prioritization
* Font size
* Row height
* Alignment
* Numeric alignment
* Currency formatting
* Status presentation
* Sorting
* Filtering
* Pagination
* Sticky headers
* Horizontal overflow
* Responsive behavior

Numbers and monetary values should be easy to scan.

Use consistent formats:

$1.82M
$425.6K
12,483
99.82%

Do not display excessive decimal precision unless business-required.

7. Status Design

Standardize statuses across Dashboard and Simulator.

Example:

READY
PROCESSING
COMPLETED
PENDING
FAILED
RETRYING
TIMEOUT
RECONCILIATION REQUIRED

Use consistent visual treatment for status badges, icons, and text.

Do not rely on color alone.

Example:

● Completed
● Processing
● Failed
● Attention Required

Ensure sufficient contrast and accessibility.

8. Simulator UX

The Simulator should feel like an enterprise operational tool, not a generic form.

Organize it into:

Scenario
   ↓
Input
   ↓
Execution
   ↓
Processing Progress
   ↓
Result
   ↓
Evidence / Details

Make it easy to:

* Create test data
* Upload input
* Select processing scenario
* Execute
* Monitor progress
* Inspect results
* Review errors
* Retry
* Reset

Avoid putting too many controls on one screen.

Use progressive disclosure for advanced options.

9. Forms

Standardize:

* Label placement
* Control height
* Required indicators
* Validation messages
* Help text
* Dropdowns
* Date/time controls
* Currency/number inputs
* File upload controls

Validation should be:

* Immediate where appropriate
* Specific
* Actionable
* Consistent

Avoid generic messages such as:

“Invalid input.”

Prefer:

“Amount must be greater than $0.00.”

10. Spacing and Density

Use a consistent spacing scale.

For example:

4px
8px
12px
16px
24px
32px

Avoid arbitrary values such as:

13px
17px
21px
27px

unless specifically justified.

Enterprise dashboards should be information-dense but not visually crowded.

11. Responsive Design

Review:

* Desktop
* Laptop
* Tablet
* Smaller screens

Ensure:

* KPI cards reflow correctly
* Tables remain usable
* Filters wrap logically
* Dialogs fit the viewport
* Text does not truncate important information
* Charts remain readable
* Navigation remains usable

Do not simply shrink fonts to make layouts fit.

Prefer reflow, grouping, collapsing, and responsive layouts.

12. Accessibility

Target WCAG 2.2 AA principles.

Review:

* Keyboard navigation
* Focus visibility
* Color contrast
* Screen-reader labels
* Semantic HTML
* Form labels
* Error identification
* Status announcements
* Tooltip accessibility
* Table semantics
* Interactive element size
* Color-independent status indicators

Do not use color as the only indication of success/failure/status.

13. Visual Consistency

Identify and fix:

* Different button heights
* Different card padding
* Different border radius
* Different heading sizes
* Different table styles
* Different status badges
* Different spacing
* Different icon sizes
* Different form control heights
* Different modal layouts

Create reusable components/tokens where duplication exists.

14. Enterprise UX Principles

Apply these principles throughout:

* Information hierarchy over decoration
* Consistency over novelty
* Readability over density
* Progressive disclosure over information overload
* Actionable errors
* Predictable navigation
* Minimal cognitive load
* Clear operational status
* Accessibility by default
* Responsive by design
* Reusable design system
* No unnecessary animations
* No decorative UI that competes with operational information

15. Do Not Over-Redesign

Preserve the application’s existing:

* Brand identity
* Color palette
* Business terminology
* Navigation model
* Functional behavior
* Existing component library

Do not introduce a completely new visual style unless the current design fundamentally prevents usability.

Focus on refinement, consistency, and enterprise quality.

16. Implementation Rules

Before changing code:

1. Identify the existing design system/theme.
2. Identify duplicate styles.
3. Identify global typography definitions.
4. Identify reusable components.
5. Identify page-specific overrides.
6. Create a remediation plan.

Then implement changes centrally wherever possible.

Prefer:

Design Tokens
     ↓
Shared Components
     ↓
Dashboard
     ↓
Simulator

instead of individually fixing every component.

Do not introduce !important unless absolutely necessary.

Do not duplicate CSS.

Do not break existing functionality.

Do not change API contracts or business logic as part of UX remediation.

17. Verification

After implementation:

* Build the application.
* Run existing unit tests.
* Run existing component/UI tests.
* Check for console errors.
* Check responsive layouts.
* Check keyboard navigation.
* Check accessibility.
* Check all Dashboard widgets.
* Check all Simulator workflows.
* Check loading/error/empty states.
* Check long numbers and large amounts.
* Check long error messages.
* Check large tables.
* Check different processing statuses.

Fix regressions before completing the task.

